# PixiJS OOP Patterns

Rules for writing robust, leak-free PixiJS classes. Based on real memory leaks and lifecycle bugs found in this project.

---

## Resource Lifecycle — The `destroy()` Contract

Every class extending `Container` must implement `destroy()` that cleans up **all** owned resources. PixiJS has no garbage collector for WebGL resources — skipping `destroy()` leaks GPU memory.

### Required Cleanup Checklist

```ts
class MyScreen extends Container {
  private timerIds: number[] = [];
  private boundHandlers = new Map<string, (...args: unknown[]) => void>();

  destroy(options?: DestroyOptions) {
    // 1. Kill all GSAP tweens targeting this or children
    gsap.killTweensOf(this);
    gsap.killTweensOf(this.children);

    // 2. Clear all timers
    this.timerIds.forEach(id => clearTimeout(id));
    this.timerIds.forEach(id => clearInterval(id));
    this.timerIds = [];

    // 3. Remove socket event listeners (with specific callback refs)
    this.stopSocketEventListeners();

    // 4. Remove DOM event listeners (with stored refs)
    this.boundHandlers.forEach((handler, event) => {
      window.removeEventListener(event, handler);
    });
    this.boundHandlers.clear();

    // 5. Null out references to large objects
    this.phaseOne = null;
    this.phaseTwo = null;

    // 6. Call super.destroy with children cleanup
    super.destroy({ children: true, ...options });
  }
}
```

### Anti-Pattern — Empty `destroy()` (DO NOT)

```ts
// BAD: Found in GameScreen.ts — delegates nothing, leaks everything
destroy(options?: DestroyOptions) {
  super.destroy(options);
}
```

### Rule
- `destroy()` MUST call `hide()` (or equivalent cleanup) before `super.destroy()`.
- `destroy()` MUST pass `{ children: true }` to `super.destroy()` to recursively clean up the display tree.
- Every `setTimeout` / `setInterval` call must store its ID and clear it in `destroy()`.
- Every `gsap.to()` / `gsap.from()` / `gsap.timeline()` must be killable — either via `gsap.killTweensOf(target)` or by storing the tween and calling `.kill()`.

