## Event Listener Hygiene

### DOM Event Listeners

**NEVER** use `.bind(this)` or anonymous arrows directly in `addEventListener`. Each call to `.bind()` creates a new function reference, making `removeEventListener` impossible.

```ts
// BAD — the two .bind(this) calls create different references, listener is never removed
window.addEventListener("message", this.handleAppMessage.bind(this));
window.removeEventListener("message", this.handleAppMessage.bind(this)); // Does nothing!

// BAD — anonymous arrow can never be removed
window.addEventListener("appMessage", (event) => { this.handle(event); });
```

**ALWAYS** store the bound reference as a class field:

```ts
// GOOD — same reference used for add and remove
class MyScreen extends Container {
  private readonly onMessage = this.handleAppMessage.bind(this);
  private readonly onAppMessage = this.handleAppMessage.bind(this);

  show() {
    window.addEventListener("message", this.onMessage);
    window.addEventListener("appMessage", this.onAppMessage);
  }

  hide() {
    window.removeEventListener("message", this.onMessage);
    window.removeEventListener("appMessage", this.onAppMessage);
  }
}
```

Alternative: use arrow function class fields (auto-bound):

```ts
class MyScreen extends Container {
  private handleMessage = (event: MessageEvent) => {
    // `this` is correctly bound
  };

  show() { window.addEventListener("message", this.handleMessage); }
  hide() { window.removeEventListener("message", this.handleMessage); }
}
```

### PixiJS Pointer Events

For PixiJS `.on()` / `.off()`, the same rule applies — store the handler reference:

```ts
// GOOD
private readonly onPointerDown = this.handlePointerDown.bind(this);

show() { this.on("pointerdown", this.onPointerDown); }
hide() { this.off("pointerdown", this.onPointerDown); }
```

### Interactivity Toggling

Prefer `eventMode` over the deprecated `interactive` property. When disabling interactivity temporarily (e.g., during animations), use `eventMode = 'none'` and restore to `'static'` — don't toggle `.interactive` on 20+ individual children.

```ts
// BAD — toggling interactive on every child individually
this.hitButton.interactive = false;
this.standButton.interactive = false;
this.splitButton.interactive = false;
// ... 10 more lines

// GOOD — disable the parent container
this.actionButtonContainer.eventMode = "none";
// Re-enable later
this.actionButtonContainer.eventMode = "static";
```

