## Screen Lifecycle Contract

All screens (classes shown via `navigation.showScreen()`) must implement this lifecycle:

```ts
interface ScreenLifecycle {
  show(): Promise<void>;    // Enter animations, start listeners, begin game loop
  hide(): Promise<void>;    // Exit animations, stop listeners, kill tweens, clear timers
  resize(w: number, h: number): void;  // Responsive layout
  destroy(options?: DestroyOptions): void;  // Full cleanup, call hide() first
}
```

### Lifecycle Flow

```
construct → show() → [active: events, animations, timers] → hide() → destroy()
```

- `show()` starts listeners and animations
- `hide()` stops everything (tweens, timers, listeners) — the screen must be inert after `hide()`
- `destroy()` calls `hide()` if not already called, then releases all resources and calls `super.destroy({ children: true })`

### Rule
Never rely on `destroy()` being called after `hide()`. `destroy()` must be self-sufficient — always call `hide()` internally as a safety measure:

```ts
destroy(options?: DestroyOptions) {
  this.hide(); // Idempotent — safe to call twice
  gsap.killTweensOf(this);
  super.destroy({ children: true, ...options });
}
```

